== fuller page ==
Pin never writes a fact alone. Each note says what was said, when it was
seen (in UTC, the world's shared clock), who or what saw it, where it came
from (a file and its lines), that file's hash at that moment, and its state
[row]. Tap any small mark in this book and those notes open.

Why "seen" and not "true since"? We can rarely know when a fact changed in
the world; we can always say when each witness saw it [seen].

C4 already keeps its own date this way. Its DATE file is written by one
tool, one line per roll, newest last: the date, the UTC time it was rolled,
the host, and the date before it [date]. Its table tool stamps each column
and row with the time the writer wrote down, kept apart from the file
system's clock; a table is current only when the two agree [stamp].

A fact seen by one hand only wears a small dagger, †, the mark Corwin's
paper on diligence uses for "claims resting only on that single witness"
[dagger]. It is a count, not a refusal: when a second, different hand writes
a row, the dagger goes. A copy of your own note does not count; "a
reflection is not a witness" [mirror].

The shop has not built the register these rows will live in yet: when this
page was read, the c4 tree had no c/seen [plan].

