== narration ==
# Take A, "the company talks to you": every voice speaks to the listener.
[picture A]
narrator: Here's Pin at the desk, with a clock on the wall and a map beside it.
spoke: I saw it! Pin, write it down.
pin: What you saw, and when, and where, and who saw it. All four, for you.
narrator: If only one friend saw it, Pin writes a small mark on it. Can you find the little mark on the card?
spoke: And when a new friend sees it too?
pin: Then the mark is gone. Two friends, no mark.
[fuller A]
pin [row]: Every note I write says what was said, when it was seen, who saw it, where it came from, and a hash of that file. Tap a mark and you'll see.
spoke [seen]: We write seen, not true since. We can't always know when a thing changed. We can always say when we saw it.
narrator [date]: {C4|C four} keeps its own date this way. One line each time: the date, the time in UTC, the computer, and the date before.
pin [stamp]: Its table tool writes down when it wrote, and keeps that apart from the file's own clock. When the two agree, the table is current.
spoke [dagger]: A fact only one hand saw gets a little dagger. It isn't a no. It's just a count, until someone else looks.
pin [mirror]: And a copy of my own note doesn't count as a second look. A reflection is not a witness.
narrator [plan]: The place these notes will live in the shop isn't built yet. We looked, and it wasn't there. So we wrote that down too, for you.

# Take B, "the lap reader": one close voice reads to you; Spoke and Pin
# mostly talk to each other, and turn to you only in a short aside.
[picture B]
narrator: Spoke sees it. Pin writes it down: what, and when, and where, and who.
spoke: Seen, Pin. Just now.
pin: Just now isn't a time, Spoke. I'll write the clock.
spoke: You wrote "before the start" for the odd one at the door. That isn't a time either.
pin: He didn't stay long enough for a clock.
narrator: If only one friend sees it, Pin writes a small mark on it.
spoke: Shall I fetch someone?
narrator: When a new friend sees it too, the mark is gone.
[fuller B]
narrator [row]: Pin never writes a fact alone. Each note says what was said, when it was seen, who or what saw it, where it came from, that file's hash at that moment, and its state.
narrator [seen]: We can rarely know when a fact changed in the world. We can always say when each witness saw it.
spoke [date]: The shop's date file is like your notes, Pin. One line per roll, newest last.
pin [date]: The date, the time it rolled, the host, and the date before. I approve.
narrator [stamp]: The table tool stamps each column and row with the time the writer wrote down, kept apart from the file system's clock.
narrator [dagger]: A fact seen by one hand only wears a small dagger: the paper's mark for claims resting only on that single witness.
spoke [mirror]: Can't you just copy it twice, Pin?
pin [mirror]: No. A reflection is not a witness.
narrator [plan]: The register these rows will live in is planned, not built. When this page was read, it was not there.

